---
import { guide, site } from "../data/site";
import ArticleLayout from "../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
---

<ArticleLayout
	description="What the SolidCraft desktop app, browser app, agent chat, and this website send over the network, and what stays on your computer."
	eyebrow="PROJECT / PRIVACY"
	title="Privacy"
>
	<p>Effective October 24, 2026.</p>
	<h2>Desktop app</h2>
	<p>
		SolidCraft has no telemetry, no accounts, and no usage reporting. Your
		designs stay on your computer unless you share them yourself.
	</p>
	<p>
		The one network request SolidCraft makes on its own is the update check. At
		startup, and again once a day while it stays open, it asks the GitHub API
		for the latest SolidCraft release. The request carries a fixed User-Agent
		and nothing else: no version number, no machine or user identifier, no usage
		data, and no cookies. GitHub sees it like any other visit to its API,
		including your IP address and the time. If you choose{" "}
		<strong>Update now</strong>, SolidCraft downloads the release files from
		GitHub releases. Turn the check off under Preferences, or set{" "}
		<code>SOLIDCRAFT_UPDATE_CHECK=0</code>. Builds you compile yourself, the
		Flatpak, and the browser app never check. Details are in{" "}
		<a href={guide("updates")}>updates and privacy</a>.
	</p>
	<p>
		Crash reports are written to a folder on your computer. SolidCraft does not
		send them anywhere. <strong>Report a bug</strong> lets you review a
		screenshot and opens a draft GitHub issue in your browser; nothing is
		published until you submit it yourself.
	</p>
	<h2>Agent chat</h2>
	<p>
		Agent chat is optional and exists only in the desktop app. It talks to the
		coding agent you run on your computer, or to the OpenAI-compatible endpoint
		you configure (a local one such as Ollama or LM Studio, or a URL you
		choose). Your prompts, any files you attach, and what the agent reads from
		your design go only to that agent or endpoint. SolidCraft does not proxy or
		provide a model and does not send them anywhere else. What an agent sends on
		to its own service is governed by that service&rsquo;s terms. An
		endpoint&rsquo;s key is kept in your system keychain and sent only to that
		endpoint.
	</p>
	<h2>Browser app</h2>
	<p>
		The browser app runs in your browser. Designs stay in the browser&rsquo;s
		private storage or in files you choose to open and save; SolidCraft does not
		upload them. The app keeps a copy of itself in your browser so it works
		offline, and checks for a newer build from the site that serves it. Clearing
		the site&rsquo;s data can remove designs kept in browser storage, so
		download a copy of anything you want to keep. See the{" "}
		<a href={guide("files")}>file guide</a>.
	</p>
	<h2>This website</h2>
	<p>
		The site has no accounts, forms, advertising, or analytics scripts, and it
		sets no cookies. Fonts, images, and videos are served from the site itself.
	</p>
	<p>
		solidcraft.io is served through Cloudflare. Like any host, Cloudflare
		processes ordinary request information such as your IP address and request
		headers, and may keep logs or aggregate traffic statistics under its own{" "}
		<a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">privacy policy</a>.
	</p>
	<p>
		The home, download, and releases pages ask api.github.com for public release
		information directly from your browser, with no credentials. GitHub receives
		your IP address and ordinary request headers. The result is kept in your
		browser&rsquo;s local storage for five minutes, and the pages work if
		storage or GitHub is unavailable. SolidCore progress pages likewise fetch
		public reports from tangency-labs.github.io. Download links go straight to
		GitHub release files, and this site adds no tracking parameters. GitHub
		handles those requests under its{" "}
		<a
			href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
		>
			privacy statement
		</a
		>.
	</p>
	<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
	<p>
		SolidCraft doesn&rsquo;t collect usage data today. If we ever add anonymous
		usage statistics, they will be opt-in, we&rsquo;ll say exactly what&rsquo;s
		collected, and we&rsquo;ll update this page (with a new effective date) and
		announce it in the release notes before shipping it.
	</p>
	<h2>Contact</h2>
	<p>
		Questions or corrections: open an issue at{" "}
		<a href={`${site.repo}/issues`}>tangency-labs/solidcraft</a>.
	</p>
</ArticleLayout>
